ate against the prior Luce baseline where useful, but treat **today's executable state as authority**.\n\nReport any drift.\n\n---\n\n# 3. BUILD THE CURRENT END-TO-END RUNTIME GRAPH\n\nTrace actual executable flow.\n\nExpected conceptual flow may resemble:\n\n```text\nWINDOWS AUDIO DEVICE\n    ↓\nsounddevice.RawInputStream\n    ↓\naudio callback\n    ↓\nVAD / silence gate\n    ↓\naudio queue\n    ↓\nAssemblyAI websocket send loop\n    ↓\nAssemblyAI transcription\n    ↓\nwebsocket receive loop\n    ↓\nfinal-turn logic\n    ↓\nfinal queue\n    ↓\ndispatcher\n    ↓\ntranscript correction\n    ↓\ncommand/dictation router\n    ↓\nkeyboard typing / command execution\n```\n\nDo not assume that remains accurate.\n\nProduce the **actual current flow**.\n\nFor every node return:\n\n```text\nCOMPONENT:\nFILE:\nFUNCTION / CLASS:\nTHREAD / TASK:\nINPUT:\nOUTPUT:\nQUEUE / SOCKET:\nTIMEOUT:\nERROR HANDLING:\nRECONNECT BEHAVIOR:\nLOGGING:\nFAILURE EFFECT:\n```\n\n---\n\n# 4. MICROPHONE DEVICE RECON — HIGH PRIORITY\n\nDon reports:\n\n> turning the physical microphone OFF and back ON does not reliably reconnect VoiceBridge.\n\nInvestigate Windows input-device behavior thoroughly.\n\nDetermine:\n\n- exact microphone device currently selected\n- whether config specifies an explicit device or `default`\n- Windows default input\n- Windows default communications input\n- device index used by `sounddevice`\n- full `sounddevice.query_devices()` inventory\n- duplicate device names\n- duplicate endpoints for the same physical microphone\n- MME / DirectSound / WASAPI / WDM-KS variants if exposed\n- host API currently used\n- whether device indices change after physical power cycle\n- whether the selected device disappears while powered off\n- whether it returns under a new device index\n- whether Windows default changes during this event\n- whether VoiceBridge retains a stale device handle\n- whether `RawInputStream` raises an exception\n- whether the callback stops firing silently\n- whether the process believes the stream is still active\n- whether audio queue activity stops\n\nReturn a compact device table.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nINDEX | DEVICE NAME | HOST API | INPUT CH | DEFAULT? | ACTIVE? | NOTES\n```\n\nDo not expose irrelevant playback devices unless useful for diagnosis.\n\n---\n\n# 5. MIC POWER-CYCLE REPRODUCTION\n\nPerform a controlled test if operationally possible.\n\nBaseline:\n\n```text\nMIC ON\n→ speak test phrase\n→ verify capture\n→ verify STT\n→ verify typed output\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```text\nMIC OFF\n→ observe VoiceBridge\n→ record exceptions/state/logs\n\nMIC ON\n→ observe whether device returns\n→ speak test phrase\n→ measure recovery\n```\n\nRecord timestamps for:\n\n```text\nT0 microphone switched OFF\nT1 callback/audio stops\nT2 microphone switched ON\nT3 Windows sees device\nT4 VoiceBridge receives audio again\nT5 STT result received\nT6 text typed\n```\n\nCalculate:\n\n```text\nOFF detection latency\nON detection latency\nreconnect latency\nspeech-to-text latency after reconnect\ntotal recovery time\n```\n\nIf it never recovers:\n\n```text\nRECOVERY = FAILED\n```\n\nIdentify the earliest layer that remains dead.\n\n---\n\n# 6. DEVICE-HANDLE / RECONNECT LOGIC\n\nInspect the source for recovery logic around:\n\n```text\nRawInputStream\nPortAudio\nsounddevice\ndevice unavailable\ncallback exception\nstream aborted\nstream stopped\ndevice disappeared\ndevice returned\n```\n\nDetermine whether VoiceBridge has any mechanism to:\n\n```text\ndetect device loss\nclose stale stream\nre-enumerate devices\nselect replacement/default\nre-open RawInputStream\nreconnect automatically\n```\n\nClassify:\n\n```text\nFULLY IMPLEMENTED\nPARTIALLY IMPLEMENTED\nABSENT\nBROKEN\nUNKNOWN\n```\n\nIf absent, document that.\n\nDo not implement it.\n\n---\n\n# 7. WINDOWS DEFAULT DEVICE QUESTION\n\nSpecifically determine whether using:\n\n```text\nmic_device = default\n```\n\nis contributing to instability.\n\nWe need evidence on whether VoiceBridge resolves the Windows default:\n\n```text\nonce at startup\n```\n\nor effectively follows:\n\n```text\nthe current default device dynamically\n```\n\nDetermine whether the implementation stores:\n\n- numeric index\n- device name\n- `None`\n- default sentinel\n- PortAudio resolved device\n\nand what happens when the endpoint disappears.\n\nDo not recommend changing to an explicit device until you establish how current resolution works.\n\n---\n\n# 8. MULTIPLE MIC LINES / DUPLICATE ENDPOINTS\n\nDon suspects Windows has too many microphone lines configured.\n\nVerify.\n\nIdentify:\n\n```text\nphysical microphone\nUSB interface/device name\nduplicate input endpoints\nvirtual microphones\ncommunications endpoints\ndisabled/disconnected devices visible to PortAudio\nBluetooth/hands-free endpoints\nwebcam microphones\nlaptop/internal microphones\nvirtual audio cables\nother VoiceBridge-visible inputs\n```\n\nDetermine which could plausibly cause VoiceBridge to reconnect to the wrong source.\n\nClassify:\n\n```text\nACTIVE CANDIDATE\nDUPLICATE\nUNRELATED\nVIRTUAL\nSTALE / DISCONNECTED\nUNKNOWN\n```\n\nDo **not** disable or remove devices.\n\n---\n\n# 9. CONTINUOUS-RUNNING HEALTH RECON\n\nVoiceBridge is intended to operate continuously.\n\nDetermine whether runtime behavior degrades over time.\n\nObserve or inspect evidence for:\n\n```text\nwebsocket age\naudio stream age\nthread health\nqueue depth\nqueue starvation\nqueue backlog\nCPU\nmemory\nopen handles\nreconnect count\nAssemblyAI session count\ncallback cadence\ndispatcher cadence\n```\n\nCheck whether any thread/task can die while the main process remains alive.\n\nEspecially inspect:\n\n```text\n_audio_callback\n_send_loop\n_recv_loop\n_dispatch_loop\nsupervisor / watchdog logic\n```\n\nFor each:\n\n```text\nCAN DIE SILENTLY?\nIS RESTARTED?\nIS HEALTH CHECKED?\nIS FAILURE LOGGED?\n```\n\n---\n\n# 10. 20-SECOND DEAD PERIOD RECON\n\nDon directly observed an approximately 20-second period where VoiceBridge stopped capturing useful speech and missed part of what he was saying.\n\nSearch logs and code for durations near:\n\n```text\n20 seconds\n15 seconds\n10 seconds\n30 seconds\n```\n\nInspect:\n\n- socket timeout\n- reconnect delay\n- retry backoff\n- endpoint timeout\n- silence timeout\n- ForceEndpoint timeout\n- supervisor interval\n- queue polling timeout\n- AssemblyAI inactivity timeout\n- PortAudio recovery behavior\n- Windows audio-device recovery\n- blocking joins\n- sleep calls\n- debounce\n\nDetermine whether the observed ~20-second gap maps to a configured or emergent timeout.\n\nDo not force a match if evidence does not support one.\n\n---\n\n# 11. LATENCY BREAKDOWN\n\nInstrument observationally where possible.\n\nWe need the latency chain:\n\n```text\nspeech begins\n→ audio callback\n→ audio queued\n→ audio sent upstream\n→ AssemblyAI final received\n→ final queued\n→ dispatcher receives\n→ router completes\n→ keyboard typing begins\n```\n\nFor several normal utterances record approximate:\n\n```text\nCAPTURE LATENCY\nUPSTREAM/STT LATENCY\nFINALIZATION LATENCY\nDISPATCH LATENCY\nROUTING LATENCY\nTYPE LATENCY\nEND-TO-END LATENCY\n```\n\nReport:\n\n```text\nMIN\nTYPICAL\nMAX\n```\n\nAlso identify outliers.\n\nWe care more about **where the lag occurs** than precise benchmark perfection.\n\n---\n\n# 12. FINALIZATION / END-OF-TURN RECON\n\nPrevious Luce work identified a possible seam around final-turn handling.\n\nReinspect current logic around:\n\n```text\nis_final\nend_of_turn\nturn_is_formatted\nForceEndpoint\n```\n\nDetermine whether speech can be recognized upstream but never enter the final queue.\n\nSpecifically test:\n\n- normal sentence\n- short sentence\n- one word\n- long dictation\n- speech followed by long silence\n- speech immediately followed by more speech\n- interrupted phrase\n- microphone power-off immediately after speaking\n\nLook for:\n\n```text\nreceived partial\nbut no final\n```\n\nor:\n\n```text\nreceived Turn\nbut rejected by final predicate\n```\n\nQuantify if possible.\n\n---\n\n# 13. VAD / SILENCE GATE RECON\n\nCurrent/previous implementation used VAD-lite behavior.\n\nVerify the current actual values.\n\nTest whether the gate can clip:\n\n```text\nquiet speech\nfirst syllable\nspeech immediately after silence\nspeech after microphone reconnect\nlow-volume microphone output\ntrailing words\n```\n\nDetermine whether:\n\n```text\nRMS threshold\nsilence duration\nqueue clearing\nstream timing\n```\n\ncan account for dropped audio.\n\nDo not alter the threshold.\n\nReturn:\n\n```text\nVAD CONTRIBUTING:\nYES / NO / POSSIBLE / UNKNOWN\n```\n\nwith evidence.\n\n---\n\n# 14. ASSEMBLYAI SESSION / WEBSOCKET HEALTH\n\nInspect the current AssemblyAI lifecycle.\n\nDetermine:\n\n```text\nwhen socket is created\nwhen socket is closed\nsession longevity\nreconnect behavior\nretry behavior\nheartbeat/ping behavior\nprovider-side session expiry\nwhether mic disconnect affects websocket state\nwhether socket stays alive while no audio is sent\nwhat happens after prolonged VAD silence\n```\n\nDetermine whether the client can reach a state where:\n\n```text\nsocket appears connected\nbut useful transcription has stopped\n```\n\nCheck logs/evidence.\n\n---\n\n# 15. AUDIO QUEUE HEALTH\n\nInspect `_audio_queue`.\n\nDetermine:\n\n```text\nqueue type\nmax size\ndrop behavior\nblocking behavior\ncleanup behavior\noverflow behavior\nwhat happens during network stalls\nwhat happens while mic is unavailable\n```\n\nLook for either:\n\n```text\nqueue growing indefinitely\n```\n\nor:\n\n```text\naudio silently discarded\n```\n\nRecord queue depth during test conditions if possible.\n\n---\n\n# 16. FINAL QUEUE / DISPATCHER HEALTH\n\nInspect `_final_queue`.\n\nDetermine:\n\n```text\nqueue type\nmax size\nconsumer behavior\nexception handling\nwhether dispatcher can die\nwhether one bad command blocks later utterances\n```\n\nTest whether utterances can accumulate and then appear late.\n\nThis is particularly important for the perceived:\n\n```text\n\"it froze and then caught up\"\n```\n\nvs:\n\n```text\n\"it froze and permanently lost speech\"\n```\n\ndistinction.\n\nClassify observed failures as:\n\n```text\nDELAYED\nDROPPED\nMISROUTED\nPAUSED\nNOT CAPTURED\nNOT FINALIZED\nUNKNOWN\n```\n\n---\n\n# 17. PAUSE / RESUME / SLEEP TERMS — VERIFY CURRENT TRUTH\n\nDo not assume the latest intended command terms were actually implemented correctly.\n\nInspect current code and tests.\n\nReturn the complete current pause/resume vocabulary.\n\nSpecifically check:\n\n```text\nsleep\nwake up\nstop listening\nstart listening\npause\nresume\n```\n\nand any aliases.\n\nFor every phrase classify:\n\n```text\nACTIVE COMMAND\nREMOVED\nALIAS\nDICTATION\nUNKNOWN\n```\n\nAlso state matching semantics:\n\n```text\nEXACT UTTERANCE\nPREFIX\nFUZZY\nREGEX\nOTHER\n```\n\nDon's desired operating behavior is:\n\n```text\nstop listening\n→ deliberate pause command\n\nstart listening\n→ deliberate resume command\n```\n\nand common normal words should not accidentally shut down VoiceBridge.\n\nBut this recon must report **what the code actually does today** before proposing correction.\n\n---\n\n# 18. IMPORTANT PAUSE SEMANTICS QUESTION\n\nEstablish exactly what \"pause\" currently means technically.\n\nDoes pause:\n\nA. keep microphone and STT streaming but ignore finals?\n\nB. stop sending audio upstream?\n\nC. stop microphone capture?\n\nD. close the AssemblyAI socket?\n\nE. stop dispatcher activity?\n\nF. some combination?\n\nThis matters because VoiceBridge is currently intended as an always-available continuous system.\n\nReturn the exact state transition:\n\n```text\nACTIVE\n↓\nPAUSE COMMAND\n↓\nWHAT REMAINS RUNNING?\n↓\nRESUME COMMAND\n↓\nWHAT HAS TO RESTART?\n```\n\nDetermine whether pause/resume itself can contribute to reconnect lag.\n\n---\n\n# 19. COMMAND ROUTER RE-VERIFICATION\n\nThe previous defect family involved command words intercepting dictation.\n\nVerify current post-fix behavior rather than trusting tests alone.\n\nAt minimum test:\n\n```text\nRight.\nRight, I think we should do that.\n\nLeft.\nLeft on the counter.\n\nUp.\nUp in the morning.\n\nDown.\nDown the street.\n\nSave.\nSave the file later.\n\nCopy.\nCopy this sentence into the notes.\n\nSleep.\nstop listening\nstart listening\n```\n\nClassify each as:\n\n```text\nCOMMAND\nDICTATION\nPAUSE\nRESUME\nDROP\n```\n\nWe need to ensure the latest routing fix did not introduce new latency or state problems.\n\n---\n\n# 20. LOGGING HEALTH\n\nInspect:\n\n```text\nbridge.log\nintent_audit.log\nany error log\nstdout/stderr destination\n```\n\nPrior recon found that `bridge.log` could become stale while intent logging continued.\n\nDetermine current state.\n\nAnswer:\n\n```text\nIS BRIDGE.LOG LIVE?\nIS INTENT_AUDIT LIVE?\nDO THEY HAVE MATCHING TIMESTAMPS?\nARE EXCEPTIONS BEING LOST?\nDO PYTHONW LAUNCHES HIDE ERRORS?\n```\n\nIf `pythonw.exe` causes important exceptions to disappear from observable logs, document that as an operational defect.\n\nDo not change launcher yet.\n\n---\n\n# 21. PYTHONW / LAUNCHER RECON\n\nConfirm current launcher:\n\n```text\nDesktop shortcut\nSTART-VOICEBRIDGE.cmd\nRESTART-KEEL-VOICE.cmd\nor anything newer\n```\n\nDetermine whether behavior differs when run under:\n\n```text\npython.exe\nvs\npythonw.exe\n```\n\nEspecially regarding:\n\n```text\nstdout\nstderr\nPortAudio errors\nwebsocket errors\ntracebacks\nreconnect messages\n```\n\nNo permanent launcher changes.\n\n---\n\n# 22. THREAD / TASK INVENTORY\n\nAt runtime list all VoiceBridge-relevant:\n\n```text\nthreads\nasync tasks\nqueues\nwebsocket tasks\naudio callbacks\nsupervisor loops\n```\n\nFor each report:\n\n```text\nNAME\nPURPOSE\nALIVE?\nDAEMON?\nRESTARTABLE?\nSUPERVISED?\nEXCEPTION VISIBILITY?\n```\n\nWe are looking for a classic failure state:\n\n> one worker dies, UI/process remains alive, Don thinks VoiceBridge is listening, but nothing useful is happening.\n\n---\n\n# 23. RESOURCE / LONG-RUNNING BEHAVIOR\n\nLook for evidence of:\n\n```text\nmemory growth\nthread accumulation\nreconnect accumulation\nsocket accumulation\nqueue accumulation\nfile-handle leakage\nCPU spikes\nPortAudio handle leakage\n```\n\nNo need for a long benchmark if historical/runtime evidence suffices.\n\nIf testing duration is limited, state that clearly.\n\n---\n\n# 24. ERROR HANDLING MAP\n\nCreate an error-handling map for:\n\n```text\naudio device lost\naudio callback exception\nqueue failure\nAssemblyAI disconnect\nAssemblyAI timeout\nSTT parse failure\ndispatcher exception\ncommand router exception\nkeyboard injection failure\n```\n\nFor every class state:\n\n```text\nLOGGED?\nRETRIED?\nRECOVERED?\nPROCESS DIES?\nTHREAD DIES?\nSILENT FAILURE?\n```\n\n---\n\n# 25. FAILURE CLASSIFICATION\n\nAfter testing, classify every observed VoiceBridge failure into one of these layers:\n\n```text\nL0 — WINDOWS AUDIO DEVICE\nL1 — PORTAUDIO / SOUNDDEVICE STREAM\nL2 — VAD / AUDIO CALLBACK\nL3 — AUDIO QUEUE\nL4 — ASSEMBLYAI SEND\nL5 — ASSEMBLYAI / NETWORK\nL6 — FINAL TURN DETECTION\nL7 — FINAL QUEUE\nL8 — DISPATCHER\nL9 — COMMAND / DICTATION ROUTER\nL10 — KEYBOARD OUTPUT\nL11 — PAUSED STATE\nL12 — LOGGING / OBSERVABILITY\n```\n\nUse these layer IDs throughout the findings.\n\n---\n\n# 26. REPRODUCTION MATRIX\n\nReturn a matrix covering at least:\n\n```text\nnormal dictation\nshort dictation\nlong dictation\nquiet speech\n20+ sec silence then speech\nmic OFF → ON\nmic OFF → ON repeatedly\npause → resume\npause → wait → resume\nlong continuous runtime\ncommand word alone\ncommand word + normal sentence\n```\n\nColumns:\n\n```text\nTEST\nEXPECTED\nACTUAL\nFAILURE LAYER\nDELAY\nDROPPED TEXT?\nRECOVERED?\nEVIDENCE\n```\n\n---\n\n# 27. ROOT-CAUSE HYPOTHESES\n\nProduce no more than **seven** ranked hypotheses.\n\nEach must include:\n\n```text\nHYPOTHESIS\nFAILURE LAYER\nEVIDENCE FOR\nEVIDENCE AGAINST\nREPRODUCED?\nHOW TO PROVE\nLIKELY FIX CLASS\nBLAST RADIUS\nCONFIDENCE\n```\n\nPotential categories may include, but are not limited to:\n\n```text\nstale audio device handle\ndevice index drift\nwrong Windows default input\nduplicate audio endpoints\nmissing reconnect supervisor\ndead audio callback\nAssemblyAI socket stale after long silence\nqueue stall\nfinal-turn timeout\nVAD clipping\ndispatcher failure\npaused-state behavior\nhidden pythonw exception\n```\n\nDo not force any of these.\n\nEvidence decides.\n\n---\n\n# 28. SEPARATE IMMEDIATE DEFECTS FROM OPTIMIZATION\n\nReturn three buckets:\n\n## P0 — FUNCTIONAL FAILURES\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nmic does not recover\nspeech permanently dropped\nworker dies silently\n```\n\n## P1 — RELIABILITY / LATENCY\n\nExample:\n\n```text\n20-second stalls\ninconsistent reconnect\nlong finalization\n```\n\n## P2 — CLEANUP / OPTIMIZATION\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nduplicate Windows devices\ndead config\nlogging cleanup\nREADME drift\n```\n\nDo not allow P2 cleanup to obscure P0/P1 failures.\n\n---\n\n# 29. MINIMUM RECOVERY / IMPROVEMENT PLAN\n\nDo not implement.\n\nRecommend a **sequenced, reversible plan**.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nFIX 1\n→ test\n\nFIX 2\n→ test\n\nFIX 3\n→ soak test\n```\n\nEach recommendation must include:\n\n```text\nPROBLEM SOLVED\nFILES / COMPONENTS AFFECTED\nEXPECTED BEHAVIOR\nRISK\nROLLBACK\nVERIFICATION TEST\n```\n\nAvoid redesigning VoiceBridge unless the current architecture makes reliable recovery impossible.\n\n---\n\n# 30. SPECIAL QUESTION — SHOULD VOICEBRIDGE BE ALWAYS STREAMING?\n\nProvide evidence, not preference.\n\nCompare the current implementation against these possible operating models:\n\n```text\nA. microphone + AssemblyAI stream permanently open\n\nB. microphone permanently open, AssemblyAI session recycled as needed\n\nC. microphone/device supervised and automatically reopened on endpoint change\n\nD. pause command only suppresses typing while capture remains healthy\n\nE. pause command actually tears down capture/STT and recreates on resume\n```\n\nState which model VoiceBridge currently implements.\n\nThen identify which observed failures are caused or worsened by that model.\n\nDo not redesign yet.\n\n---\n\n# 31. REQUIRED LUCE RETURN\n\nReturn exactly these major sections:\n\n```text\nGCE VOICEBRIDGE — FULL RUNTIME HEALTH RECON RETURN\n\n1. EXECUTIVE STATUS\n2. LIVE CURRENT STATE\n3. CURRENT END-TO-END RUNTIME GRAPH\n4. WINDOWS AUDIO DEVICE INVENTORY\n5. ACTIVE MICROPHONE BINDING\n6. MIC OFF/ON REPRODUCTION\n7. DEVICE RECONNECT LOGIC\n8. CONTINUOUS-RUNTIME HEALTH\n9. 20-SECOND STALL ANALYSIS\n10. LATENCY BREAKDOWN\n11. VAD FINDINGS\n12. ASSEMBLYAI SESSION HEALTH\n13. AUDIO QUEUE HEALTH\n14. FINALIZATION / FINAL QUEUE HEALTH\n15. DISPATCHER HEALTH\n16. CURRENT PAUSE / RESUME VOCABULARY\n17. CURRENT PAUSE STATE MACHINE\n18. COMMAND ROUTER RE-VERIFICATION\n19. LOGGING / OBSERVABILITY HEALTH\n20. THREAD / TASK HEALTH\n21. RESOURCE / LONG-RUNNING FINDINGS\n22. ERROR-HANDLING MAP\n23. REPRODUCTION MATRIX\n24. VERIFIED FAILURE LAYERS\n25. ROOT-CAUSE HYPOTHESES\n26. P0 / P1 / P2 CLASSIFICATION\n27. MINIMUM REVERSIBLE FIX PLAN\n28. UNKNOWN / NEEDS RECONCILIATION\n29. LUCE VERDICT\n```\n\n---\n\n# 32. LUCE VERDICT FORMAT\n\nFinish with:\n\n```text\nLUCE VERDICT\n\nVOICEBRIDGE CURRENTLY:\n[HEALTHY / DEGRADED / BROKEN / INTERMITTENT / UNKNOWN]\n\nNORMAL DICTATION:\n[PASS / FAIL / INTERMITTENT]\n\nMIC OFF → ON RECOVERY:\n[PASS / FAIL / INTERMITTENT]\n\nACTIVE MIC DEVICE:\n<device>\n\nDEVICE BINDING MODE:\n[DEFAULT / EXPLICIT / OTHER]\n\nDUPLICATE MIC ENDPOINT RISK:\n[YES / NO / UNKNOWN]\n\n20-SECOND STALL REPRODUCED:\n[YES / NO / PARTIAL]\n\nDROPPED SPEECH REPRODUCED:\n[YES / NO / PARTIAL]\n\nEARLIEST VERIFIED DROP LAYER:\n<L0-L12 or UNKNOWN>\n\nCONTINUOUS-RUNTIME HEALTH:\n[PASS / DEGRADED / FAIL / UNKNOWN]\n\nASSEMBLYAI SESSION HEALTH:\n[PASS / DEGRADED / FAIL / UNKNOWN]\n\nVAD CONTRIBUTING:\n[YES / NO / POSSIBLE / UNKNOWN]\n\nAUDIO QUEUE HEALTH:\n[PASS / DEGRADED / FAIL / UNKNOWN]\n\nDISPATCHER HEALTH:\n[PASS / DEGRADED / FAIL / UNKNOWN]\n\nCURRENT PAUSE PHRASE:\n<actual code truth>\n\nCURRENT RESUME PHRASE:\n<actual code truth>\n\nBARE \"SLEEP\" CURRENTLY DOES:\n<actual behavior>\n\nBARE \"WAKE UP\" CURRENTLY DOES:\n<actual behavior>\n\nRECOMMENDED PAUSE VOCABULARY:\n<only after reporting current truth>\n\nTOP P0 DEFECT:\n<finding>\n\nTOP P1 DEFECT:\n<finding>\n\nSAFE TO HAND TO NICO:\n[YES / NO]\n\nRECOMMENDED FIRST SURGICAL FIX:\n<single smallest action>\n\nCONFIDENCE:\n<0-100%>\n```\n\n---\n\n# 33. FINAL OPERATING RULE\n\nDo not confuse:\n\n```text\nVoiceBridge process is running\n```\n\nwith:\n\n```text\nVoiceBridge is healthy.\n```\n\nA process that remains alive while:\n\n- its microphone stream is dead\n- its websocket is stale\n- a worker thread has died\n- utterances are being dropped\n- it is stuck paused\n- or the wrong Windows endpoint is bound\n\nis a **failed VoiceBridge runtime**.\n\nThis recon must establish **functional liveness at every stage**, not merely process liveness.\n\nGround the complete runtime.\n\nReproduce what can be reproduced.\n\nIdentify the earliest failing layer.\n\nThen hand Don a surgical recovery sequence.\n\n**No fixes during recon.**\n:::\n\nThis is the one I’d run before we touch VoiceBridge again. It should tell us whether we really have **one bug or several stacked together**—especially whether the mic power-cycle issue is Windows/PortAudio while the lag/drop issue is further downstream."]}, "create_time": 1790405760.137885, "end_turn": true, "id": "13e76ed8-bdd2-4657-bb1d-184667f9683c", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [{"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 337, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id": "3cad0b9c-c2aa-475e-bf13-c79aab0d62df", "cited_message_idx": 29, "cloud_doc_url": null, "doi": null, "effective_time": null, "journal_homepage_url": null, "journal_name": null, "library_file_id": "libfile_0ff357eaf20c819193c84b3d7d5efef6", "line_range": [16, 35], "publication_year": null, "retrieval_file_index": 0, "retr