-4aba-8688-b136237289a3", "working_turn_id": "698b10a2-16cd-4aba-8688-b136237289a3"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790406998.233601, "weight": 1}, {"author": {"metadata": {}, "name": null, "role": "assistant"}, "channel": "final", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Yep — this recon is extremely useful. We’ve got a much cleaner picture now, and there are **three distinct VoiceBridge problem families**, not one.\n\n### What Luce proved\n\n**1. Mic OFF → ON failure has a real root cause.** VoiceBridge opens the Windows default input once via `RawInputStream(device=None)` and then holds that PortAudio stream for its lifetime. When the USB mic disappears, `_running` remains `True`, so the supervisor incorrectly believes capture is healthy and never rebuilds the stream. The UI can literally continue saying “listening” while VoiceBridge is permanently deaf. fileciteturn1file0L103-L122\n\nThat is a **P0 bug**, and it’s very surgical.\n\n**2. Your “too many Windows mic lines” suspicion is correct.** The same USB PnP microphone has been enumerated multiple times by Windows (`2-`, `3-`, `5-` instances) and then mirrored across MME, DirectSound, WASAPI, and WDM-KS. Luce found **six VoiceBridge-visible endpoints representing the same physical USB mic**, plus Realtek/internal inputs and a loopback device. fileciteturn1file0L65-L88\n\nImportant nuance: the duplicates aren’t what kill the live connection. The bigger issue is that after a reconnect, VoiceBridge asks Windows for “default” again, so it can silently attach to a different endpoint. fileciteturn1file0L123-L134\n\n**3. The sleep language is actually correct now.** The current code has exactly:\n\n```text\nstop listening → pause\nstart listening → resume\n```\n\nBoth are standalone exact matches. Bare `sleep` and `wake up` now type as normal dictation. The command-collision fix is also active: `Right, if you need me...` types normally instead of firing RIGHT-arrow. fileciteturn1file0L22-L28\n\nSo we **do not need to touch sleep terminology again right now**.\n\n### The bigger reliability problem Luce uncovered\n\nThe current supervisor is not supervising **actual health**. It is supervising a Boolean flag. Several critical components can die while `_running` remains `True`: the audio callback, send loop, receive loop, and dispatcher. fileciteturn1file0L147-L156\n\nThat explains why this thing can feel like:\n\n> “It says it’s running, but it just fucking stopped hearing me.”\n\nThere is also a second version of the same bug on the AssemblyAI side. If the websocket closes, `_recv_loop` does **not** clear `_running`, so the supervisor again sees a supposedly healthy system and never reconnects. fileciteturn1file0L263-L280\n\nThat is likely much closer to the **long-run / intermittent deafness problem** than simple latency.\n\n### There are also real silent-drop paths\n\nLuce identified three ways recognized or captured speech can disappear before typing:\n\n- formatted/final-turn logic can reject a transcript and never buffer it;\n- stopping/muting can close the audio stream before buffered audio flushes;\n- VAD can drain the entire audio queue when the gate closes. fileciteturn1file0L219-L239\n\nThe VAD deserves scrutiny. Current threshold is `500`, while a 3-second idle probe measured about `0.5`. That does **not prove** your voice is below threshold, but it proves the gate is aggressive and completely silent operationally: if it closes, `_drain_audio()` throws away queued audio. fileciteturn1file0L240-L262\n\n### And the fucking logs are lying by omission\n\nThis may be the most important engineering finding.\n\nThe application calls `logger.info()` for exactly the events we need to understand:\n\n- capture started\n- websocket closed\n- AssemblyAI session terminated\n- pause/resume/mute\n\n…but the logging configuration is not actually initialized in the normal import path, so those INFO events are invisible. The log also lacks useful timestamps for parts of the flow. fileciteturn1file0L157-L179\n\nSo right now VoiceBridge can die internally and leave us almost no forensic trail.\n\n---\n\n## My proposed repair order\n\nI **would not optimize anything else yet**. I’d give Nico four tightly separated repair slices:\n\n1. **Functional liveness / mic recovery**  \n   Detect a dead audio stream, clear health state, re-enumerate devices, reopen the intended microphone automatically.\n\n2. **WebSocket liveness recovery**  \n   Any terminated send/recv loop or closed AssemblyAI socket must make capture unhealthy so the supervisor reconnects.\n\n3. **Observability**  \n   Fix logging initialization, add timestamps, and expose capture/socket/queue health. This is what lets Luce prove later stalls instead of guessing.\n\n4. **Then evaluate VAD/finalization**  \n   Measure your actual speech RMS before changing `500`; then investigate the final-turn discard behavior.\n\nI would **not** start by deleting Windows microphone devices. Windows cleanup might be worthwhile later, but the application should survive device disappearance and re-enumeration regardless.\n\nAlso, Luce’s report itself says the prompt got truncated after §14, so we never got the full queue/dispatcher/error-map sections from the original recon request. fileciteturn1file0L1-L5 That’s okay—the evidence here is already strong enough to authorize the first two surgical fixes.\n\n**My next move would be a Nico handoff specifically for “VoiceBridge Runtime Liveness Phase 1” — mic recovery + real health supervision + websocket recovery, while explicitly leaving VAD and latency untouched.**"]}, "create_time": 1790406998.23607, "end_turn": true, "id": "0d3a271c-98fc-4cb4-b8c5-f067e7331293", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [{"citation_format_type": "berry_file_search", "end_ix": 624, "metadata": {"extra": {"api_tool_source": "files/context_stuff", "authors_display": null, "cited_message_id":